title:    Thirty minutes later, with nobody touching anything
subtitle: Three trials. Poll every ~13s across each expiry boundary.
width:    1000
---
# Each trial: approve a grant, then poll the folder's IAM policy until the
# binding disappears. Times are relative to accessGrantTime + 1800s.

  trial 1   last seen bound  -6s      first seen gone  +7s
  trial 2   last seen bound  -6s      first seen gone  +7s
  trial 3   last seen bound  -7s      first seen gone  +6s

# Removal lands inside a ~13-second window around the documented expiry,
# in all three runs. There is no meaningful period where the grant has
# ended and the access has not.
#
# The window is the POLL INTERVAL, not the precision of the result. Two
# pollers watching the same trial-2 expiry reported 7s and 13s purely
# because their polls landed at different moments. "Within ~13 seconds"
# is what this method supports; a single-second figure is not.

$ gcloud pam grants list ... --format="value(state)"
ENDED

$ gcloud resource-manager folders get-iam-policy folders/<FOLDER> \
    --impersonate-service-account=jit-access-demo@<SEED>.iam.gserviceaccount.com

ERROR: (gcloud.resource-manager.folders.get-iam-policy)
  [jit-access-demo@<SEED>.iam.gserviceaccount.com] does not have permission
  to access folders instance [<FOLDER>:getIamPolicy] (or it may not exist):
  The caller does not have permission.

# Byte-for-byte the error from figure 01. No cleanup job, no destroy,
# no human. The denial came back on its own.
